# Release Process (Team Falcon)

## Schedule
- Production releases go out every Thursday at 14:00 IST.
- No releases on the last Friday of a quarter (code freeze).
- Hotfixes can ship any weekday before 18:00 IST.

## Branch naming
- Release branches are named `release/YYYY.MM.N`, for example `release/2026.11.2`.
- Hotfix branches are named `hotfix/<ticket-id>`, for example `hotfix/FAL-1423`.

## Approvals
- Every release needs approval from the Release Captain for that week.
- The Release Captain rotates weekly; the current list lives in the #falcon-release Slack channel.
- A release needs 2 approvals on the release PR: the Release Captain and one QA engineer.

## Steps
1. Cut the release branch from `main` on Wednesday by 17:00 IST.
2. Run the full regression suite (`nightly-regression` job); it must be green.
3. Deploy to staging and run smoke tests for at least 2 hours.
4. Get approvals, then deploy to production on Thursday at 14:00 IST.
5. Post release notes in #falcon-release within 1 hour of deploying.

## Rollback
- If error rate rises above 2% within 30 minutes of a release, roll back immediately.
- Rollback command: `falconctl rollback --to previous`.


# On-call Guide (Team Falcon)

## Rotation
- Each on-call shift lasts 7 days, starting Monday at 10:00 IST.
- Two people are on call at a time: a primary and a secondary.
- The schedule is managed in PagerDuty under the service name `falcon-platform`.

## Response times
- Sev1 (service down): acknowledge within 5 minutes, start working on it within 15 minutes.
- Sev2 (major feature broken): acknowledge within 30 minutes.
- Sev3 (minor issue): handle during working hours, within 2 working days.

## Escalation
1. Primary on-call gets paged first.
2. If the primary does not acknowledge in 10 minutes, the secondary is paged.
3. If neither acknowledges in 20 minutes, the Engineering Manager, Priya Nair, is paged.
4. For Sev1 incidents lasting more than 1 hour, open an incident channel named `#inc-YYYYMMDD-<short-name>`.

## Communication
- Post status updates in #falcon-oncall every 30 minutes during a Sev1.
- Write a postmortem for every Sev1 within 3 working days.

## Handover
- Handover meeting is every Monday at 09:30 IST.
- The outgoing primary writes a handover note listing open incidents and known risks.


# Test Guidelines (Team Falcon)

## Coverage
- Minimum line coverage for every service is 82%.
- New code in a PR must have at least 90% coverage.
- PRs that drop overall coverage by more than 0.5% are blocked by CI.

## Test types
- Unit tests live in `tests/unit/` and must run in under 2 minutes in total.
- Integration tests live in `tests/integration/` and run only on the `main` branch and release branches.
- End-to-end tests run in the `nightly-regression` job at 02:00 IST.

## Naming rules
- Test files: `test_<module>.py`.
- Test functions: `test_<what>_<expected_result>`, for example `test_login_with_bad_password_returns_401`.
- CI job names: `<service>-<test-type>`, for example `payments-unit` or `payments-integration`.

## Flaky tests
- A test that fails and then passes on retry without code changes is marked flaky.
- Flaky tests must be tagged `@pytest.mark.flaky` and get a ticket in the FLAKY Jira project.
- A flaky test must be fixed or deleted within 10 working days.
- If a service has more than 5 open flaky tickets, it cannot be released until they are below 5.

## Test data
- Never use production data in tests.
- Shared fixtures live in `tests/conftest.py`.

